**Sec. 50101**
This section repeals specific Inflation Reduction Act provisions to restore prior onshore oil and gas royalty rates and noncompetitive leasing rules, while mandating the Secretary of the Interior to immediately resume quarterly lease sales. The Secretary must conduct at least four lease sales per fiscal year in nine specified states (including Wyoming, New Mexico, and Alaska), offering at least 50 percent of nominated available parcels in each state. The bill defines "eligible lands" broadly to include all lands not statutorily excluded from leasing and requires lease sales to occur within 18 months of receiving an expression of interest. Additionally, it amends the Mineral Leasing Act to limit drilling permit validity to a single non-renewable four-year period and mandates approval of production commingling applications under specified measurement conditions.

**Sec. 50102**
The Secretary of the Interior is required to conduct a minimum of 30 region-wide offshore oil and gas lease sales in the Gulf of America region and six sales in the Alaska Cook Inlet Planning Area between 2025 and 2040. Gulf of America sales must offer at least 80 million acres per sale with royalty rates set between 12.5 and 16.67 percent, while Alaska sales must offer at least 1 million acres per sale. Beginning in fiscal year 2034, 70 percent of revenues from Alaska offshore leases will be paid to the State of Alaska, with the remaining 30 percent deposited into the U.S. Treasury. The section also repeals Inflation Reduction Act royalty provisions, caps offshore royalty rates at 16.67 percent, and increases the annual limit on distributed Gulf of Mexico Energy Security Act revenues to $650 million for fiscal years 2025–2034 and $500 million for 2035–2055.

**Sec. 50103**
This section repeals Section 50263 of the Inflation Reduction Act, which previously established a methane emissions fee on extracted methane from oil and gas operations on federal and Indian lands.

**Sec. 50104**
The Secretary of the Interior must conduct at least four area-wide oil and gas lease sales in the Alaska Coastal Plain within ten years, offering at least 400,000 acres per sale based on the 2020 Record of Decision terms. The initial sale must occur within one year of enactment, with subsequent sales scheduled at two-year intervals. Revenue sharing for these leases will provide 50 percent of adjusted receipts to the State of Alaska for fiscal years 2025–2033, increasing to 70 percent for fiscal year 2034 and thereafter, with the balance deposited into the U.S. Treasury.

**Sec. 50105**
The Secretary of the Interior is directed to restore and resume oil and gas lease sales in the National Petroleum Reserve-Alaska (NPR-A) based on the 2020 Integrated Activity Plan, requiring at least five sales within ten years. Each sale must offer at least 4 million acres, with the initial sale occurring within one year of enactment and subsequent sales every two years. Beginning in fiscal year 2034, 70 percent of receipts from these leases will be paid to the State of Alaska, while 30 percent will be deposited into the U.S. Treasury.

**Sec. 50201**
Within 90 days of enactment, the Secretary of the Interior must process qualified coal lease applications by publishing environmental reviews, establishing fair market value, and holding lease sales to identify the highest bidder. The Secretary may also grant additional approvals for previously issued coal leases to allow mining activities to commence.

**Sec. 50202**
This section amends the Mineral Leasing Act to reduce the federal coal royalty rate from 12.5 percent to a maximum of 7 percent for leases issued before, on, or after the enactment date until September 30, 2034. Lessees who have already paid advance royalties at the higher rate are entitled to a credit for the difference between the amount paid and the amount required under the new lower rate.

**Sec. 50203**
The Secretary of the Interior must make at least 4 million additional acres of known recoverable coal resources on federal land available for lease within 90 days of enactment. This mandate excludes federal lands within National Monuments, National Recreation Areas, Wilderness Preservation System components, Wild and Scenic Rivers, National Trails, National Conservation Areas, Wildlife Refuges, Fish Hatcheries, and National Park System units.

**Sec. 50204**
The Secretary of the Interior is authorized to permit the mining of federal coal reserves adjacent to state or private land if necessary for economic viability, provided the mining plan was previously approved. The Secretary must take steps to authorize this mining within 90 days of enactment, subject to National Environmental Policy Act review.

**Sec. 50301**
The Secretary of Agriculture must increase annual timber sales on National Forest System land by at least 250 million board-feet each fiscal year from 2026 to 2034, while the Secretary of the Interior must increase sales on Bureau of Land Management public lands by at least 20 million board-feet annually during the same period. The Forest Service must enter into at least 40 long-term timber contracts of at least 20 years, and the Bureau of Land Management must enter into at least 5 such contracts, with all revenues deposited into the general fund of the Treasury.

**Sec. 50302**
The Secretaries of the Interior and Agriculture must collect annual acreage rents and capacity fees from holders of rights-of-way for wind and solar energy projects on federal and National Forest System land. Acreage rent is calculated using a per-acre rate, an encumbrance factor (100% for solar, at least 10% for wind), and a 3% annual adjustment factor, while capacity fees are the greater of the acreage rent or 3.9% of gross electricity proceeds. Wind projects may qualify for a 10% capacity fee reduction if at least 25% of the land is used for other activities, and rights-of-way may be terminated if payments are more than 90 days late.

**Sec. 50303**
Beginning January 1, 2026, 25 percent of revenues from renewable energy projects on covered federal land will be paid to the state where the revenue is derived, and 25 percent will be allocated to counties based on the percentage of land from which the revenue is derived. These payments are in addition to existing payments in lieu of taxes and must be made available in the fiscal year following collection.

**Sec. 50304**
This section rescinds the unobligated balances of funds made available to the National Park Service and Bureau of Land Management under Sections 50221, 50222, and 50223 of the Inflation Reduction Act of 2022.

**Sec. 50305**
The Secretary of the Interior, acting through the National Park Service, is appropriated $150 million for fiscal year 2025 to fund events and activities commemorating the 250th anniversary of the founding of the United States, with funds remaining available through fiscal year 2028.

**Sec. 50401**
The Department of Energy is appropriated $218 million for maintenance and repairs of Strategic Petroleum Reserve facilities and $171 million for purchasing petroleum products for storage, with funds available until September 30, 2029. This section also repeals the mandate requiring the drawdown and sale of Strategic Petroleum Reserve oil established by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017.

**Sec. 50402**
This section repeals Section 50142 of the Inflation Reduction Act and rescinds the unobligated balances of funds made available under Sections 50123, 50141, 50144, 50145, 50151, 50152, 50153, and 50161 of the Inflation Reduction Act.

**Sec. 50403**
The Energy Policy Act of 2005 is amended to expand the scope of loan guarantees to include projects that increase capacity, support grid reliability, and enable energy and critical mineral development, with $1 billion appropriated for fiscal year 2025 for these activities. The commitment authority for the Inflation Reduction Act’s clean energy financing section is extended from 2026 to 2028.

**Sec. 50404**
The Secretary of Energy is directed to mobilize National Laboratories to curate scientific data for artificial intelligence models and initiate efforts to develop self-improving AI models for science and engineering, with $150 million appropriated for these activities through September 30, 2026. The curated data and AI models are intended to accelerate the development of next-generation microelectronics and new energy technologies via the American science cloud.

**Sec. 50501**
The Secretary of the Interior, acting through the Commissioner of Reclamation, is appropriated $1 billion for fiscal year 2025 to restore or increase the capacity of existing Bureau of Reclamation conveyance and surface water storage facilities. These funds remain available through September 30, 2034, are not subject to reimbursement or cost-sharing requirements, and contracts entered into under this section are not treated as new or amended contracts for certain statutory purposes. [end of text]


